2 ARCHITECTURE

The instruction set architecture of STM was designed from scratch;
it has no compatibility for any existing CPUs.  Its basic ideas were
taken from today's mainstream RISC architectures, such as MIPS, SPARC,
Alpha and PowerPC, though some CICSy concepts from IBM370, VAX,
68k and x86 were also taken into account.

If the processor is to be implemented with FPGA, its size must be kept
fairly small; say, less than 50K gates.  This requirement strongly
influenced the architecture decision (as well as the implementation,
as we will see later).

Since STM is basically RISC, its architectural features are described
here in terms of the differences from other existing RISCs.

1) 16 GRs of 32-bit width

Register files occupy a considerable amount of chip size in any
processor.  Due to the size limitation, STM could not afford 32 GRs
or 64-bit width, which many today's RISCs are equipped with.

Simply reducing the number of GRs would cause a register allocation
problem to a processor with load-store architecture.  To solve this
problem, the next feature was introduced.

2) Register-Memory Operation

STM doesn't adopt load-store architecture; it supports 
"Reg <- Reg <op> Mem" type of instructions that many CISCs have
(herein called "mem-op instructions").  With mem-op insns, you
don't have to load every operands into register, thus reducing
the number of regs required.  Most CISCs have 16 GRs (x86 has 8),
so I think 16 is enough.

Mem-op insns do require some additional hardware (roughly one more
ALU), but it is still considerably less than another 16 regs.

Having smaller number of regs does have its merits other than size:
	o  shorter access time,
	o  less overhead for task switching,
	o  fewer bit fields in insn format, etc.

3) Has Condition Code

STM has CC, like SPARC & PPC and unlike MIPS & alpha.  It just happend
to be so:-); on second thought I might well have designed an arch
without CC.  This point may be changed in the future.

4) No Delayed Branch

Historically, delayed branch was first introduced by 1st generation
RISCs, mainly for hardware convenience.  However, this is by no means
a nice thing from a software standpoint, and it is not as effective 
in the era of superscalar as it was in the late 80's.  Some recent
RISCs such as alpha do not adopt delayed branch.  Neither does STM.

5) No FPU

Right now, I have PC-type applications in mind as possible usage of
STM (though it may extend to broader fields in the future).  I don't
see keen necessity for FPU now.  Besides, there's the size problem
again.  I may consider FPU implementation in the future.

6) Big Endian

It just happend to be so; sorry, DEC and intel folks:-)  It's just
I'm more used to big-endian archs.


